{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"The transport-corrected execution (bbff5b4cad8f47129ee5997bb6eee815, exit 0, all four tests passed, no parse loss) closes the event finding's last measurement limitation. In one run: `default_capacity_parked_cluster` {event_capacity 1024 — the shipped default left untouched, nodes_parked 2, decided_before 1129 = decided_after 1129, append_notify_timeout_reached true} shows the bound reached at the default and the healthy node unable to move the decided index; `parked_majority_cluster` (capacity 8) repeats the cluster-level stall under the same corrected transport; `unconsumed_event_bound_reached`/`actor_parked`/`append_notify_endpoint_withheld` restate the node-local freeze at capacity 64; and the two drained-stream events remain the control. The account now cites that single receipt for both scopes, the 'bounds only 64/8, default argued from source' limitation is withdrawn, the superseded harness failure (run 8f27d10f: a per-sender forwarding task jammed behind the parked destination and stalled with only one node parked) is retained explicitly as an excluded cause, and the remaining limits are the honest ones: client-side trigger with no documented drain obligation, a dropped receiver unblocks the actor, and the parked-set size is schedule-dependent (at least one node in case 1, two of three in the cluster cases). The other two findings — the core's missing accept-phase repair for a lost Accepted and append_notify resolving Ok for an index occupied by another entry — keep their recorded accounts, receipts and limits.\"}"}}
